<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Jambi Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1615 CE – 1904 CE (approx.) · capital Jambi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Pepper-exporting Malay sultanate on the Batang Hari river of Sumatra, whose last great sultan fought the Dutch for nearly half a century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Jambi stood on the site of ancient <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/melayu-kingdom">Malayu</a>, but the Islamic sultanate took shape in the early 17th century. By 1615 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and English traders had opened factories there to buy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> grown in the upriver highlands, and the court used this wealth to rival <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/johor-sultanate">Johor</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palembang">Palembang</a> for control of the trade of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The sultan presided over a riverine state in which downstream nobles controlled trade while upstream communities, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minangkabau-people">Minangkabau</a> settlers, grew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a>. Jambi fought a long war against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/johor-sultanate">Johor Sultanate</a> and sacked its capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batu-sawar">Batu Sawar</a> in 1673. The decline of pepper prices after about 1680 weakened the court.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> imposed treaties in the 19th century and deposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-thaha-saifuddin">Sultan Thaha Saifuddin</a> in 1858. He waged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> resistance from the interior until he was killed in 1904, after which Jambi was annexed into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/dutch-east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a> and later became a province of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/indonesia">Indonesia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/jambi-sultanate.jpg" alt="Jambi Sultanate" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Ham105 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:White_flag_3_to_2.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1615 CE – 1904 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Jambi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sultan Thaha Saifuddin</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Jambi Malay</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sultanate of Jambi</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1760&amp;focus=jambi-sultanate" aria-label="Jambi Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7557 2239 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Jambi Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7877 2495L7959 2663"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7959 2663h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7877 2495h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7877 2495h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Jambi. <a href="/atlas?year=1760&amp;focus=jambi-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

